t are  involved in disorders and not  necessarily bathing the entire bodies  and pharmaceuticals another example of  imprecise applications of course is in  the field of genetics and throughout the  field of genetics what we're learning is  that medical categories that the  categories like that we use like bipolar  disorder schizophrenia autism depression  and so on then these are broad labels  but they actually encompass many  different biological disorders that have  different genetic signatures in autism  for example we know that there's a  variety of genetic mutations that  contribute to vulnerability to autism  and some of them have shared biology and  some of them may not and that means that  the therapies of the future are going to  have to address this specific disorder  that someone has and function who's  developed the revolutionary new tool  known as CRISPR is is that uses  developed this technology to develop  those treatments for specific  genetically defined disorders in both as  a research tool but also we're now  seeing increasingly even in clinical  trials using CRISPR related technologies  that are going after RNA editing in  adults for example and I think over the  over the next few years we'll be seeing  more and more clinical applications of  this technology  in a related way Fung is using these  CRISPR related methods to detect  disease-causing agents and detective  ulnar abilities to disease so for  example he can use and what is now is a  very inexpensive easy to apply  technology that can be used out in the  field it could use in developing  countries to detect and fetch infectious  agents like as Ebola Zika for example  and then and as well as disease-causing  mutations and cancer and mutations that  lead to vulnerabilities and I think this  kind of approach is going to have a big  impact on healthcare in the upcoming  years as well co-op being funky we'll be  hearing from later is taking these  CRISPR based technologies and using them  to 