  and Jim DeCarlo was taking this work  even a step further in developing these  artificial models which have properties  that resemble those of the neurons that  he records in the primate brain so for  example he will develop take these  models train them on massive databases  of images like you see the chairs here  and they learn to recognize these and  then he will find the models whose have  properties that resemble those of the  neurons that he studies and primates and  but of course the question that is often  raised by this kind of work is well but  what's the biological relevance of these  models I mean how do we know that this  artificially developed model actually  resembles how the brain actually does  these same computations so in recent  work he's done a really fantastic test  of this idea in that he has taken these  models that have developed these neural  properties that seemed to resemble those  that he finds in his recordings and he  said okay so if this is a really  biologically relevant model we should be  able to use the model to predict what  will happen if we show  oh the monkey and therefore these  neurons images that have they've never  seen before and we will predict the  neurons response from the model and and  so he'll take this for example the the  models prediction of the neural activity  caused by the cat and he can look at  this activity across the population this  that bar graph up top is just an  activity measure recorded across a group  of cells and one of the things he's  asked is well let's say that we want to  make one neuron in this population given  a more selective response and so it'll  respond more and its neighbors will  respond less can we use the model to  predict what would that image be and it  turns out that this image down here on  the right what seems like a bit of  nonsense but the network came up with  that and it predicted that if you were  to show the monkey that pattern it would  give you a more precise activation  pattern in the population and indeed  that's what he finds the the the the  actual population records resembles what  the model predicts suggesting that we're  on the right track now one of the other  I think think major insights that Pat  had was that these problems that we've  been talking about understanding the  brain and helping people with brain  disorders and so on  is going to require an international  effort it's not all going to be done  here and so Pat and Laurie with the help  of Hugo Xiang decided that they would  establish brain Institute's in China who  they felt we're going to be making a  major contribution to neuroscience in  the future and they established these  three McGovern institutes in Beijing who  are all represented here today and we'll  be hearing more from them and we've now  developed this Cooperative Research  Center in Shenzhen that's developing the  genetically altered primates for example  and and we've seen now this developing  of this international network for  neuroscience that I think is already  beginning  to pay off and neuroscience discoveries  that we'll be hearing today and I think  one of the things that's really  encouraging about the development of the  neuroscience of the Chinese Institutes  is that they're following this same  multidisciplinary concept that we're  applying here in other words they're  studying the mind and brain across  multiple levels of scale many people are  sitting at the intersections of  different techniques molecular learning  systems and so on and they're  establishing Institute's where people  can then talk all these across all these  levels and I think we have become  confident that these kinds of  interactions across people will enable  us to understand the interactions in the  brain and and now I would say Pat if  you've been listening I hope that you  accept this this progress that we've  been making from what you got started  you and Lori started here at the  McGovern Institute and th